;; Minimal, namespace-scoped lint config.
;;
;; The library's intentional shadowing of clojure.core is NOT handled here — it is
;; declared in core.clj's own ns form via (:refer-clojure :exclude [...]), which
;; silences the compiler and the linter alike and doubles as a machine-checked
;; manifest of what is shadowed on purpose. :redefined-var is deliberately left ON
;; so an accidental redefinition still gets caught as wrappers are added.
{:config-in-ns
 {;; The test ns refers :all from clojure.test and from consistent-lib.core so
  ;; that each test calls the wrapper under the same bare name clojure.core uses.
  ;; Enumerating the :refer list would have to be re-edited on every new wrapper,
  ;; so :refer-all is off for the test namespace only. Lint-only; there is no
  ;; compiler equivalent to fix at the source.
  ;;
  ;; :type-mismatch is off here for a separate, subtler reason. On a COLD cache
  ;; clj-kondo lints the test file before it has analysed src/consistent_lib/core.clj,
  ;; so the :refer :all names fall back to clojure.core and clojure.core's built-in
  ;; arg-type specs get applied to our deliberately arg-swapped wrappers. Every
  ;; resulting error is a false positive — e.g. (partition [1 2 3 4] 2) is reported
  ;; as "Expected: integer, received: vector" against clojure.core's [n coll] order.
  ;; The same run on a warm cache reports nothing, so without this the gate's verdict
  ;; depended on whether .clj-kondo/.cache happened to exist. Confirmed still needed
  ;; after core.clj gained (:refer-clojure :exclude ...) — that fixed the compiler
  ;; warnings and :redefined-var, but not this. See stage-01 report, Deviation 3.
  consistent-lib.core-test {:linters {:refer-all {:level :off}
                                      :type-mismatch {:level :off}}}}}
